    title: 'Menopause: getting your sleep back and looking after your mood',
    summary: 'Insomnia, waking in the night, irritability, anxiety or low mood around the menopause: understanding what is happening, what can help, and when to seek care.',
    body_md: `## In brief
- Around the menopause, sleep can be disturbed by **night sweats**, but also **directly by hormonal changes**.
- Lack of sleep affects **alertness**, **concentration**, **memory** and **mood**.
- **Mood swings, irritability, anxiety** are common; **depressive episodes** can also occur, especially if you have had them before.
- A sleep routine, physical activity and social contact help; **cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT)** is effective against insomnia.
- **Sadness or loss of interest that last** should prompt you to seek care without waiting.

## Understanding
Fluctuations in oestrogen and progesterone influence sleep and emotions. To this are added hot flushes that wake you, tiredness that builds up and, often, a busy period of life: teenage children or children leaving home, ageing parents, pressure at work. Santé.fr (the official French health information site) reminds us that these personal upheavals can make mood disorders worse.

That said, depression should not automatically be put down to the menopause: it deserves to be recognised and treated as such. Santé.fr specifies that in perimenopause, hormone treatment may be considered case by case, notably in the event of a depressive state with no other identified cause.

## Day to day
- **Keep regular bedtimes** and wake-up times, even at weekends.
- **Prepare a cool bedroom**, light nightwear, a suitable duvet; keep cool water by the bed.
- **Make dinner lighter** and avoid alcohol and caffeine in the evening.
- **Move during the day**: regular physical activity helps sleep and reduces anxiety.
- **If you cannot sleep**, rather than staying in bed for hours brooding, get up for a calm activity and go back to bed when tiredness returns.
- **Give yourself moments of relaxation**: relaxation, breathing, meditation, yoga, whichever suits you.
- **Keep in touch**: social life, conversations with other women going through the same stage, talking to your loved ones about how you feel.
- **Note your sleep and mood** in the app: you will see better what helps you, and you will be able to talk about it precisely with your doctor.

## Frequently asked questions
**Are sleeping pills a solution?** If insomnia persists, see someone: Santé.fr cites CBT among the effective treatments. Any medicines are discussed with your doctor.

**I get angry over nothing. Is it the menopause?** Irritability is common before and during the menopause, linked to hormones and lack of sleep. If it weighs on your life or your relationships, talk about it.

**My memory lapses worry me.** Lack of sleep can reduce concentration and memory. If these difficulties persist or get worse, take stock with your doctor.

**Can hormone treatment help?** It can improve sleep by reducing hot flushes, but it is decided case by case, according to the benefits and risks.

## When to seek advice
See someone if you have been sleeping badly for several weeks, if you feel sad, anxious or without desire most of the time for more than two weeks, or if your mood weighs on your family or work life. If you have dark thoughts or want to die, call **3114** (the French national suicide-prevention helpline), free, day and night. If you are in immediate danger, call **15** (SAMU, the French emergency medical service) or **112**.

_This article is for information only; it does not replace the advice of a doctor, a midwife or a gynaecologist._`,
